

-> What is a 'bitmask' in cp terms?
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  A bitmask is just an integer where each bit(0/1) represents some yes/no information.

  Example for n = 5
    
    we have elements 0 1 2 3 4 5

    A mask like = (10110)base2 means :->
       bit 0 = 0 -> element 0 not present
       bit 1 = 1 -> element 1 present
       bit 2 = 1 -> element 2 present
       bit 3 = 0 -> element 3 not present
       bit 4 = 1 -> element 4 present

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Basic bit operations for int : ->
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    -> check if i-th bit is set   =   mask & (i << i) != 0

    -> set i-th bit  =  mask |= (1 << i)

    -> unset i-th bit = mask &= ~(1 << i)

    -> toggle i-th bit = mask ^= (1 << i)

Treat mask as a set of indices or a subset of {0 , , n-1}

